WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.980 --> 00:00:06.990
My name is Devin.

00:00:06.990 --> 00:00:09.168
I'm a solutions
engineer at Posit,

00:00:09.168 --> 00:00:10.960
and I'm really thrilled
to be up here today

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:13.990
to talk about Quarto
because in many ways,

00:00:13.990 --> 00:00:17.260
the vision of Quarto that
reproducible research is

00:00:17.260 --> 00:00:21.070
a core part of high quality
scientific communications

00:00:21.070 --> 00:00:23.770
was a key driver and
actually nudging me

00:00:23.770 --> 00:00:25.870
along my career
journey that ultimately

00:00:25.870 --> 00:00:28.320
has brought me to Posit.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:30.600
Almost 10 years ago
as a graduate student,

00:00:30.600 --> 00:00:33.420
R Markdown
blew me away when

00:00:33.420 --> 00:00:35.190
all of a sudden,
I could shortcut

00:00:35.190 --> 00:00:37.770
an enormous amount of
the tedious interactions

00:00:37.770 --> 00:00:40.260
from working in our
scripts and actually taking

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.220
the outputs of those, getting
it them into Word or PDF

00:00:44.220 --> 00:00:46.710
documents to distribute.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.140
Now as many graduate
students are inclined to do.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.070
I also dabbled in
figuring out how I could

00:00:53.070 --> 00:00:54.730
use the tools I was learning.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.830
In particular are
in fun new ways.

00:00:59.830 --> 00:01:04.012
So about that time, a new
tool emerged called Git book.

00:01:04.012 --> 00:01:05.470
And it was really
fascinating to me

00:01:05.470 --> 00:01:08.350
because the idea was to take
a bunch of Markdown documents,

00:01:08.350 --> 00:01:11.230
jam them through this
processor and out popped

00:01:11.230 --> 00:01:14.140
a website that had
navigation and styling

00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:16.100
and it kind of
looked like a book.

00:01:16.100 --> 00:01:19.820
And so I said, hmm, that
seems kind of interesting.

00:01:19.820 --> 00:01:22.510
I wonder if I can take
R Markdown documents,

00:01:22.510 --> 00:01:25.570
get them to Markdown to
put them through Git book.

00:01:25.570 --> 00:01:28.630
And so I put together
some really, really scary

00:01:28.630 --> 00:01:30.340
looking code.

00:01:30.340 --> 00:01:32.860
But thankfully, the
RStudio team was also

00:01:32.860 --> 00:01:36.330
keeping an eye on such ideas.

00:01:36.330 --> 00:01:40.090
And bookdown,
entered the scene.

00:01:40.090 --> 00:01:43.480
This ability to build on a
unit of an R Markdown document

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:47.230
into more complex content felt
like a taste of opportunities

00:01:47.230 --> 00:01:50.010
to come.

00:01:50.010 --> 00:01:52.650
And so similarly, when
I started playing around

00:01:52.650 --> 00:01:54.960
with what it was going to
be like to build a blog,

00:01:54.960 --> 00:01:57.570
I found Hugo, a
static site generator

00:01:57.570 --> 00:02:01.630
that you could also make most
of your content with Markdown.

00:02:01.630 --> 00:02:04.870
And so by using that content
and Markdown plus a little bit

00:02:04.870 --> 00:02:06.910
of metadata and
configuration, you

00:02:06.910 --> 00:02:11.330
could generate a
professional looking site.

00:02:11.330 --> 00:02:16.470
Again the RStudio team
responded with blogdown.

00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.350
And it was so exciting to
me because there's actually

00:02:19.350 --> 00:02:22.380
official tools to support
these janky workflows that I

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:23.920
was trying to come up with.

00:02:23.920 --> 00:02:26.460
That integration between
Hugo and Markdown

00:02:26.460 --> 00:02:30.230
was able to help
bring content to life.

00:02:30.230 --> 00:02:32.930
But as fascinating
as these experiments

00:02:32.930 --> 00:02:36.290
were, as fascinating as
learning those tools were,

00:02:36.290 --> 00:02:39.740
it was undeniable that there
were some significant points

00:02:39.740 --> 00:02:43.490
of friction and frustration
in figuring it out, especially

00:02:43.490 --> 00:02:45.470
around the tooling boundaries.

00:02:45.470 --> 00:02:48.440
It was never fun to see a Hugo
theme that just kind of didn't

00:02:48.440 --> 00:02:50.390
work for how you
wanted to lay it out

00:02:50.390 --> 00:02:52.130
or something that
worked in bookdown

00:02:52.130 --> 00:02:54.960
that you couldn't
put into blogdown.

00:02:54.960 --> 00:02:57.780
And so, as powerful
as these tools were,

00:02:57.780 --> 00:03:01.230
it was clear that the separation
of the communities that we're

00:03:01.230 --> 00:03:04.680
trying to meld together
left some gaps to make

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:06.340
this approachable to everyone.

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.680
But here we are today.

00:03:11.680 --> 00:03:13.960
And, as Mine and
Julie have explained,

00:03:13.960 --> 00:03:17.320
and hopefully as everyone
is getting so excited about,

00:03:17.320 --> 00:03:21.130
Quarto builds on the
learnings of the past decade.

00:03:21.130 --> 00:03:24.250
It unifies teams across
language, boundaries

00:03:24.250 --> 00:03:27.160
and procedural differences.

00:03:27.160 --> 00:03:29.980
And in the same
vein, Quarto can also

00:03:29.980 --> 00:03:33.820
unify the way you consider
building those books, blogs,

00:03:33.820 --> 00:03:37.510
websites, or all sorts
of other complex content.

00:03:37.510 --> 00:03:40.120
So you can focus
on enabling users

00:03:40.120 --> 00:03:44.200
to discover, consume
and navigate in the ways

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.420
that you'd like to help them.

00:03:46.420 --> 00:03:49.480
And by doing so in a
single unified tool chain,

00:03:49.480 --> 00:03:52.180
it really does give you
an unparalleled ability

00:03:52.180 --> 00:03:54.880
to control the
processing and ultimately

00:03:54.880 --> 00:03:59.520
the production of the content
that you're working on.

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:02.770
Now, one thing that some
of you may be thinking,

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:05.120
is this seems like a lot.

00:04:05.120 --> 00:04:07.350
One tool that can do so much.

00:04:07.350 --> 00:04:10.770
How may I even start
learning all of the things

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.300
that it can do?

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:14.820
So today I'm going
to do something

00:04:14.820 --> 00:04:18.240
that hopefully the presentation
gods will be with me

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:20.579
and I'm going to show
you just how easy it

00:04:20.579 --> 00:04:24.270
is to get going with Quarto
and do some pretty cool stuff

00:04:24.270 --> 00:04:26.760
and hopefully also
demonstrate about some

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:28.320
of the thoughtfulness
that the Quarto

00:04:28.320 --> 00:04:30.455
team has put into its design.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:39.500
So they switch over.

00:04:39.500 --> 00:04:41.745
So I can actually
see what I'm typing.

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:46.510
Oh, man.

00:04:46.510 --> 00:04:47.235
Where's my mouse?

00:04:50.390 --> 00:04:55.250
In I'm hoping that's
going to give it back.

00:04:55.250 --> 00:04:56.690
All right.

00:04:56.690 --> 00:04:58.690
I want to mirror.

00:04:58.690 --> 00:05:02.450
And we're in business.

00:05:02.450 --> 00:05:03.870
All right.

00:05:03.870 --> 00:05:08.710
So let's drop into RStudio.

00:05:08.710 --> 00:05:12.280
Now, one of the things that
you can always do in RStudio

00:05:12.280 --> 00:05:15.310
is if you want to go play around
with a somewhat recent version

00:05:15.310 --> 00:05:18.760
of our Quarto, you can go
in, create a new directory,

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:21.440
and Quarto is front and center.

00:05:21.440 --> 00:05:23.870
But the Quarto team
has wanted to make sure

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:27.350
that Quarto is available, even
if you're not in using rstudio,

00:05:27.350 --> 00:05:30.140
if you're using VS Code
or any other editor

00:05:30.140 --> 00:05:34.040
and so many to all the things
that you can do in the IDE,

00:05:34.040 --> 00:05:36.050
you can also do on
the command line.

00:05:36.050 --> 00:05:39.410
In this case, we want to
get going with a website.

00:05:39.410 --> 00:05:41.960
So in this case, I
can from the terminal

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:45.370
say quarto create project.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:48.350
Type equals website.

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:51.830
And what this is going to do is
start me off with a scaffold.

00:05:51.830 --> 00:05:53.960
It has a couple of
these qmd files

00:05:53.960 --> 00:05:56.120
that you've been hearing about.

00:05:56.120 --> 00:05:58.910
And it has this quarto HTML.

00:05:58.910 --> 00:06:02.960
This is the magic, the
ability to say I have the area

00:06:02.960 --> 00:06:04.520
that I'm going to
write my content,

00:06:04.520 --> 00:06:07.580
and I have a hub where
I can control how I want

00:06:07.580 --> 00:06:10.080
that content to be rendered.

00:06:10.080 --> 00:06:12.240
So if I want to
take this content,

00:06:12.240 --> 00:06:14.820
maybe add a little bit
more structure to it.

00:06:14.820 --> 00:06:20.880
So here is a header
and maybe we'll.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.480
Add a list, some Markdown.

00:06:26.480 --> 00:06:29.530
Some more interesting stuff.

00:06:29.530 --> 00:06:30.680
And I can render it.

00:06:33.420 --> 00:06:37.360
And what's going to
pop up is my site.

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:39.420
Now let's look at this
site really briefly.

00:06:39.420 --> 00:06:40.660
I have a title.

00:06:40.660 --> 00:06:44.490
I have a home and an about page.

00:06:44.490 --> 00:06:47.188
You can see my header that I
can actually link to in case,

00:06:47.188 --> 00:06:48.730
I want to get someone
directly there.

00:06:48.730 --> 00:06:52.380
If it's a longer page and I
even have a table of contents,

00:06:52.380 --> 00:06:53.500
if I have more elements.

00:06:53.500 --> 00:06:58.150
So I can see how I would
navigate through that page.

00:06:58.150 --> 00:07:01.650
Now you could go build
this today in blogdown.

00:07:01.650 --> 00:07:03.060
No problem.

00:07:03.060 --> 00:07:06.120
But where things start to
get exciting to me in quarto

00:07:06.120 --> 00:07:09.600
is just how flexible
you can be when you say,

00:07:09.600 --> 00:07:11.850
what if I want to
move things around?

00:07:11.850 --> 00:07:14.470
So in this case, you can
see how this is laid out.

00:07:14.470 --> 00:07:17.360
I have my website, I have a
title that I could change.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.850
I also have a bar, so that
top strip is a navbar.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:23.820
But if I instead said, I
want to move my content

00:07:23.820 --> 00:07:26.070
so it's on the left
right, which is actually

00:07:26.070 --> 00:07:28.930
one of the big things that would
differentiate for like a book.

00:07:28.930 --> 00:07:30.220
It's quite simple.

00:07:30.220 --> 00:07:33.730
I could just say
this is a sidebar.

00:07:33.730 --> 00:07:36.160
And I want to take
some contents.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:41.350
And if I go back here and
I want to regenerate it.

00:07:41.350 --> 00:07:46.240
My site now has no bar,
and instead my contents

00:07:46.240 --> 00:07:47.040
are on the side.

00:07:49.550 --> 00:07:52.320
You don't have to
decide up front.

00:07:52.320 --> 00:07:53.260
Is this a book?

00:07:53.260 --> 00:07:54.210
Is this a blog?

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.180
Because Quarto is
giving you the ability

00:07:56.180 --> 00:07:58.160
to put things in
the way you want

00:07:58.160 --> 00:08:00.260
and you can combine
the ideas that

00:08:00.260 --> 00:08:03.950
make books great, blogs great
or any other different type

00:08:03.950 --> 00:08:04.800
of layout.

00:08:04.800 --> 00:08:07.730
And much of that is
accessible directly

00:08:07.730 --> 00:08:10.720
with a couple lines of code
in this configuration file.

00:08:14.140 --> 00:08:17.770
And so I'm going to bring back
the navbar for a little bit.

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:20.740
And let's talk about
blogs, because I

00:08:20.740 --> 00:08:23.362
think that's one entry
point for a lot of people

00:08:23.362 --> 00:08:25.570
where you can start to dabble
with things that you're

00:08:25.570 --> 00:08:26.150
learning.

00:08:26.150 --> 00:08:28.210
You know, maybe it's
stuff at the conferences,

00:08:28.210 --> 00:08:30.340
maybe it's some of your
own personal learnings

00:08:30.340 --> 00:08:32.350
that you want to
put up to the world.

00:08:32.350 --> 00:08:34.659
And so what I want to do
is I want to add some more

00:08:34.659 --> 00:08:36.830
organization to this content.

00:08:36.830 --> 00:08:41.039
And so I'm going to
make a post directory.

00:08:41.039 --> 00:08:43.679
And in that post
directory, I'm going

00:08:43.679 --> 00:08:47.120
to make a Quarto document.

00:08:47.120 --> 00:08:50.033
My first post.

00:08:50.033 --> 00:08:51.450
Now, one of the
things that you're

00:08:51.450 --> 00:08:54.900
going to see coming up right
here is the visual editor.

00:08:54.900 --> 00:08:58.920
Now, this isn't directly
relevant to or directly enabled

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.140
only by Quarto, but
this is something

00:09:01.140 --> 00:09:04.860
that to me is so exciting
about the blend of what Quarto

00:09:04.860 --> 00:09:09.600
offers, plus what the RStudio
IDE can also bring to the table.

00:09:09.600 --> 00:09:13.440
So for some people, the
ability to write Markdown

00:09:13.440 --> 00:09:15.250
is fine for text.

00:09:15.250 --> 00:09:17.460
But as soon as you start
doing more complex things

00:09:17.460 --> 00:09:19.525
like dealing with images,
it becomes tedious.

00:09:19.525 --> 00:09:20.650
Remember, oh, what is that?

00:09:20.650 --> 00:09:23.470
Is exclamation point bracket
or is it parentheses?

00:09:23.470 --> 00:09:24.610
I can't keep track.

00:09:24.610 --> 00:09:25.900
How do I get stuff in?

00:09:25.900 --> 00:09:29.130
And suddenly going back to
a Google Doc or something

00:09:29.130 --> 00:09:32.280
feels a lot more
approachable with Quarto

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:33.580
and the visual editor.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:34.412
You get that.

00:09:34.412 --> 00:09:35.620
What you see is what you get.

00:09:35.620 --> 00:09:38.820
Interface if you want that
now, I can always switch back

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:41.070
to the source if I'm
comfortable writing that,

00:09:41.070 --> 00:09:43.770
but the visual editor has
some pretty nice goodies

00:09:43.770 --> 00:09:45.510
hidden inside of it.

00:09:45.510 --> 00:09:49.910
For example, if I wanted
to clear all of this out.

00:09:49.910 --> 00:09:52.740
So let's just take
this away real quick.

00:09:52.740 --> 00:09:57.300
To do and say I'm
here at NETCONF.

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:00.686
Our RStudio has just rebranded.

00:10:03.410 --> 00:10:04.380
And you know what?

00:10:04.380 --> 00:10:09.470
I think it would be great
to also add a screenshot,

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:12.440
so I'm going to save
this into my posts.

00:10:12.440 --> 00:10:16.666
I'm going to say my
conference experience.

00:10:19.490 --> 00:10:24.830
And let's go over to the Corps
or the new posit website.

00:10:24.830 --> 00:10:26.840
And for fun and games,
I'm going to whoops,

00:10:26.840 --> 00:10:28.080
that went pretty fast.

00:10:28.080 --> 00:10:31.160
I'm going to grab a little
screenshot maybe boom,

00:10:31.160 --> 00:10:32.790
I grabbed that screenshot.

00:10:32.790 --> 00:10:35.220
I'm going to copy
it to my clipboard.

00:10:35.220 --> 00:10:37.530
Now before the visual editor.

00:10:37.530 --> 00:10:38.773
I'd have to save this out.

00:10:38.773 --> 00:10:40.190
I'd have to put
it in a directory,

00:10:40.190 --> 00:10:42.740
I'd have to move that around.

00:10:42.740 --> 00:10:46.620
I much prefer just pasting it.

00:10:46.620 --> 00:10:49.230
And the visual editor
has now taken that

00:10:49.230 --> 00:10:53.340
and stuck it in an
image directory for me.

00:10:53.340 --> 00:10:55.740
These quality of
life improvements

00:10:55.740 --> 00:10:59.790
are all throughout
the Quarto ecosystem.

00:10:59.790 --> 00:11:01.560
To showcase another
one real quick.

00:11:01.560 --> 00:11:04.960
If I look here and I wonder,
you know, oh, what was it?

00:11:04.960 --> 00:11:06.010
Was it side bar?

00:11:06.010 --> 00:11:09.630
Was it side something I can
start typing and with some tab

00:11:09.630 --> 00:11:14.140
completion, it's going to
tell me what my options are.

00:11:14.140 --> 00:11:18.080
Now notice this isn't a long
list of every possible option.

00:11:18.080 --> 00:11:23.180
It's just the options relevant
for that level of indentation.

00:11:23.180 --> 00:11:25.250
In this case, I could
say true or false,

00:11:25.250 --> 00:11:28.400
or if I indent again
and then ask and say,

00:11:28.400 --> 00:11:30.800
tell me what things I
could do with the sidebar.

00:11:30.800 --> 00:11:33.350
You see this come up right
here and I can say, Oh yeah,

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.180
I could put some contents in.

00:11:36.180 --> 00:11:39.060
The things that could
frustrate you and make you

00:11:39.060 --> 00:11:41.430
feel like you needed
to pull your hair out.

00:11:41.430 --> 00:11:44.370
The IDE and Quarto and
the tooling built around

00:11:44.370 --> 00:11:47.370
it is trying to help you
not have to deal with that

00:11:47.370 --> 00:11:50.930
and be able to focus
on your content.

00:11:50.930 --> 00:11:55.300
And so I have this first kind
of conference experience,

00:11:55.300 --> 00:11:56.420
a page here.

00:11:56.420 --> 00:11:58.730
And what I'm going
to do is I'm also

00:11:58.730 --> 00:12:02.090
going to go kind of steal
just for the sake of time,

00:12:02.090 --> 00:12:05.450
another kind of post starter
that I grabbed right off

00:12:05.450 --> 00:12:06.870
of the website.

00:12:06.870 --> 00:12:09.235
So we can also stick
this in the Posit's page.

00:12:14.410 --> 00:12:16.730
All right.

00:12:16.730 --> 00:12:20.260
So what we have here

00:12:20.260 --> 00:12:23.570
is I've added another,

00:12:23.570 --> 00:12:27.200
more complex Quarto document
that has a bunch more

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:28.470
stuff going on to it.

00:12:28.470 --> 00:12:33.260
And the majority of the intent
of this doesn't matter so much.

00:12:33.260 --> 00:12:35.760
This is on the court's website,
so you can explore about it.

00:12:35.760 --> 00:12:37.820
But the point is, is
I'm starting to build up

00:12:37.820 --> 00:12:39.960
multiple blog posts.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:43.162
And so how are you going to
display those for your user?

00:12:43.162 --> 00:12:44.870
If we think about what
a blog might have,

00:12:44.870 --> 00:12:47.040
you might have something
that's sorted by date.

00:12:47.040 --> 00:12:49.670
It shows an author,
maybe an image preview.

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:53.570
It gives that stuff to you so
you can see what's going on.

00:12:53.570 --> 00:12:57.890
And so instead of making
it look like it's magical

00:12:57.890 --> 00:13:00.650
and I can remember how to do
this off the top of my head,

00:13:00.650 --> 00:13:03.860
this is a great opportunity to
go over to the courthouse site

00:13:03.860 --> 00:13:07.400
to say if this was the first
time I was considering trying

00:13:07.400 --> 00:13:10.160
to build a blog and I
wanted to go look at it,

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:13.410
I can go and look
through the search.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:17.370
And Quarto is going to give you
a whole bunch of information

00:13:17.370 --> 00:13:20.350
on that site about what
to do for blogging.

00:13:20.350 --> 00:13:22.990
And so in this case, I can
scroll down a little bit

00:13:22.990 --> 00:13:26.320
and they say, hey, if you
are dealing with a blog,

00:13:26.320 --> 00:13:27.860
you can grab this.

